  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify the type from the equation: x³ → cubic, 1/x → reciprocal, aˣ → exponential",
      "Cubic: S-shaped curve, can have 1 or 3 roots",
      "Reciprocal: two curved branches, never crosses the axes",
      "Exponential: rapid growth (or decay if 0 < a < 1), y-intercept always 1",
      "Substitute key values to plot: x = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2"
    ],
    "title": "Cubic, Reciprocal & Exponential Graphs",
    "content": "<p><strong>Cubic:</strong> \\(y = ax^3 + ...\\) has an S-shape. If \\(a > 0\\), it goes from bottom-left to top-right.</p><p><strong>Reciprocal:</strong> \\(y = \\frac{a}{x}\\) has two branches in opposite quadrants. Never touches the axes (asymptotes at \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 0\\)).</p><p><strong>Exponential:</strong> \\(y = a^x\\) (where \\(a > 1\\)) grows rapidly. Always passes through \\((0, 1)\\). Asymptote at \\(y = 0\\).</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Sketch \\(y = \\frac{3}{x}\\).</strong></p><p>When \\(x = 1\\), \\(y = 3\\). When \\(x = 3\\), \\(y = 1\\). When \\(x = -1\\), \\(y = -3\\). Two branches in Q1 and Q3.</p>"
  },
